docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:6:  - docs/brainstorms/2026-06-17-twofield-water-compliant-density-projection-requirements.md
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:9:# Re-architect the realism core to two-velocity-field PB-MPM (stiff grid, no Poisson) — requirements
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:13:The coffee-pour water must **bounce/scatter on impact** and read as real water, while keeping the two assets the current two-field solver has — the **coffee/water coupling** and the **frame rate** (real-time, ~200k particles / ≤33 ms). A long patching campaign on the two-field grid solver (surface-weighted dissipation transfer, density-relief/density-target pressure, merge discriminator) did **not** deliver realistic water. The converged diagnosis: **a bounce is a stiff-incompressibility (pressure) response — the pool must spike pressure and shove the impacting water back** — and the two-field's **under-converged velocity-divergence (`∇·v`) Jacobi projection is fundamentally soft** (it's fast *because* it under-converges, which is the same reason it can't bounce). A fully-converged grid Poisson would bounce but is slow (`main`'s sparse-RBGS Poisson runs 40–100 red/black pressure pairs and its pressure-pair loop plausibly dominates the frame — exact share pending a checked-in profiler artifact, see Sources).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:15:**Decision: re-architect the realism core to two-velocity-field PB-MPM** (Position-Based MPM — the SOTA fan-out's candidate **A**). PB-MPM keeps everything **on the grid** (the faster path measured here today: two-field runs 25 ms@207k on a full coupled scene vs the XPBD particle core's ~90 ms@200k *extrapolated water-only with optimization levers still open* — see R2/KD8 for why this proves "grid is faster today," not that particle XPBD is eliminated) and replaces the soft `∇·v` projection with a **compliant, position-based density constraint** (the XPBD/PBF mechanism). The bet is that this gives **stable local stiffness at a few iterations — enough for a visible bounce/push-back — without a full fine-grid Poisson**. It does **not** claim to deliver globally-converged stiff incompressibility for free: low-frequency density information still has to propagate across the domain, which is why KD2 carries an explicit **coarse-grid pressure pre-pass** (an approximate global low-frequency solve — cheaper than a full Poisson, but global pressure work nonetheless, to be measured not assumed). XPBD is the in-repo existence proof that few-iteration compliant position constraints bounce in real time. The coffee/water **coupling is rebuilt** on this substrate: two co-located momentum fields, a pore-packing density target, Laibe-Price drag, XPBI bed plasticity, and conserved absorption — patterns proven in pieces across XPBD and the two-field. Whether the constraint multiplier `λ` can be converted into a usable pore-pressure field for buoyancy/Terzaghi is a **hypothesis to validate (KD5)**, not a settled mechanism. The assembled two-velocity *porous* PB-MPM has no shipped reference (EA SEED's PB-MPM is single-phase water); that novelty is a **risk to retire by building**, separate from the validated repo physics it ports.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:21:- **Bounce = stiff incompressibility.** Water bounces/scatters off the cup/pool only if the fluid is stiff enough that an impact spikes pressure and reflects the incoming momentum. XPBD and `main` MPM bounce; two-field does not — it "just pools where it is" (visual oracle). Empirically, every two-field water failure (mushy/no-bounce, over-pack, over-buoyant coffee, settled stirring) traces to its `∇·v` projection: it's under-converged (residual compressibility), it doesn't even constrain density, and the projection dissipates the impact momentum that should bounce.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:22:- **The perf↔stiffness trap on the classical grid.** Converge the Poisson → stiff but slow; under-converge → fast but soft. Two-field chose fast/soft. `main` chose a converged-ish RBGS Poisson (40–100 red/black pressure pairs; the pressure-pair loop plausibly dominates its frame — soften pending a checked-in profiler artifact, see Sources) with an operator-inconsistency divergence floor. Neither gives bounce **and** speed.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:23:- **PB-MPM relaxes (not escapes) the trap.** It replaces the soft `∇·v` projection with a **compliant position-based density constraint** (XPBD-style): the compliance gives a *stable, stiff restoring force* on density error at a few iterations (→ local push-back → visible bounce) without converging a fine-grid solve. This buys **stable local stiffness**, not globally-converged incompressibility — the EA PB-MPM work itself notes Jacobi-style parallel constraint solving converges slowly / stays artificially soft, and presents *stability at any stiffness*, not exact stiffness, as the win. Globally-stiff 3D incompressibility still needs low-frequency information to cross the domain; KD2's coarse-grid pre-pass supplies that as **approximate global low-frequency pressure work** (cheaper than a full fine Poisson, but not free, and to be measured). The net perf claim is that this is cheaper than `main`'s fine RBGS Poisson, *not* that global pressure work disappears. EA SEED ships the single-phase core real-time in WebGPU.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:24:- **The grid is the perf path here.** Measured in-repo: two-field grid = 25.4 ms@207k; XPBD particle core = ~90 ms@200k (linear after the reorder, levers unpulled). Staying on the grid (PB-MPM) keeps the frame-rate asset; a particle core would have to claw back ~3.6×.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:25:- **The coupling must survive the move.** Coffee/water coupling (infiltration/saturation, buoyancy, crater, Terzaghi, conserved absorption) is an asset and is conservation-critical. It is the part PB-MPM must **rebuild** (the largest scope + novelty risk), because PB-MPM's incompressibility is position-based (it emits position/displacement corrections), not the cell-centered `∇p`/`G·p` the two-field coupling consumes.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:29:- **KD1 — Position-based compliant density constraint replaces the `∇·v` projection.** It targets **density** (J / volume) directly, so it cannot suffer the two-field's density-blind over-pack, and the compliance gives a *stable* stiff restoring force at a few P2G→solve→G2P iterations. Expected outcome: stable local stiffness sufficient for a visible bounce/push-back — **not** a guarantee of globally-converged stiff incompressibility at that iteration budget (a parallel Jacobi-style constraint solve converges slowly across the domain; bounce is a local response, global incompressibility is not). Globally-stiff behavior is reconciled with speed by KD2's coarse pre-pass, not by the constraint alone. (Existence proof for the *local* claim: XPBD/PBF bounces real-time today with this mechanism.)
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:30:- **KD2 — Stay on the grid; port EA SEED's WebGPU PB-MPM as the single-phase water reference, plus a coarse-grid pressure pre-pass.** Honors the frame-rate asset and gives a concrete reference for the water core. The pre-pass is an **approximate global low-frequency pressure solve** (a small multigrid-ish / coarse-level helper) — it is global pressure work, just on a coarse level, so the perf claim is "cheaper than a full fine-grid Poisson," not "no global solve." Its structure, level count, iteration count, and measured cost are an open planning question (see Outstanding Questions). This is the one substantively "unbuilt piece."
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:32:- **KD4 — Mixture incompressibility: water density target `ρ₀·(1−φ_s)` (pore-packing).** Per-cell volume fractions `φ_s` (solid) and pore fraction `n = 1−φ_s`; saturation `s = φ_f/n`. Water packs into the pore space rather than expelling grains. (Proven in XPBD's coupling — `targets ρ₀·(1−α_s)`.)
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:33:- **KD5 (HYPOTHESIS, not assertion) — `λ` may be convertible into a pore-pressure field.** *Correction:* XPBD does **not** take `∇λ` of a smooth grid field. In `src/solvers/xpbd/coupling.wgsl`, it forms a per-particle scalar `pressure_for_water(i) = max(-lambda[i], 0)` (coupling.wgsl:228-230) and then accumulates **pairwise SPH-gradient impulses** over neighbor water/grain pairs (`spiky_grad`, coupling.wgsl:257-258, :274+) — particle-side, compression-negative, not a cell-centered pressure gradient. So the original "exactly as XPBD derives buoyancy from `∇λ`" was wrong. The real decision: a PB-MPM per-cell multiplier *may* be usable as an algorithmic pore-pressure for buoyancy/Terzaghi **only after** a sign / units / smoothness / iteration-dependence / stress-partition audit (the 2026-06-17 doc already warned PBF `λ` is particle-side and compression-negative, not directly portable to the grid pressure contract). If the audit fails, buoyancy falls back to an XPBD-style pairwise impulse from `max(-λ,0)` or an explicit hydrostatic term. The over-buoyant-coffee issue is *expected* to resolve here as physical buoyancy, but that is the thing to prove, not assume.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:35:- **KD7 — XPBI for the bed plasticity/crater.** Klar et al. Drucker-Prager return map run inside the position-based loop (XPBI, arXiv 2405.11694) — pressure-dependent yield, tamping memory, the crater that holds. Plus a small APIC affine momentum on the pour/free-water to sustain the cavity (the one thing pure position-projection dissipates).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:36:- **KD8 — Perf is proven FIRST, and the prototype must bound the *assembled* system, not just single-phase water.** The first build is a minimal EA SEED single-phase water PB-MPM that measures **both the bounce and the frame rate at ~200k** before coupling. But a single-phase ≤33 ms result does **not** bound the full solver, which adds a second velocity field, drag, the coarse pre-pass, bed plasticity, wetting, extra storage buffers, and extra dispatches. So KD8 also requires a **two-velocity cost projection** alongside the prototype: either a stubbed second field + drag + λ ping-pong + bed/wetting buffer budget measured for per-pass cost, or a conservative additive estimate of each added pass's cost against the headroom. The go/no-go bar is the *projected assembled* frame time clearing ≤33 ms@200k, not the single-phase number alone.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:37:- **KD10 — Storage-buffer and fixed-point budgets are decided up front, not discovered.** The device requests **exactly 9** storage buffers per stage (`src/utils/gpu.rs:114`, `NEEDED_STORAGE_BUFFERS=9`), and gpu.rs explicitly warns that requesting more / deriving from `adapter.limits()` makes `request_device` **fail in the browser** — so anything above 9 needs a deliberate `GpuContext` limit-change *and* live browser verification (the 16 figure in `AGENTS.md` is the hardware cap, not what's currently granted; two-field's widest entry point binds 7, `common.wgsl:9`). The plan must carry a **buffer/pass ledger** for two momentum fields + `λ` (ping-pong) + bed state against both the current 9 and the sanctioned 16, splitting passes if needed. Likewise **fixed-point headroom**: the existing FP_SCALE/overflow math (`common.wgsl:124`) is for mass/momentum accumulation lanes, *not* accumulated position corrections / PB-MPM displacement state — a new overflow surface that needs its own headroom gate.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:42:- **R1 — Water bounces/scatters on impact** (crown/rebound off the cup floor, walls, and standing pool), lively free surface, not "pools where it is." Primary oracle: the live webapp on the comparison scenes. Quantitative gate (non-gameable): a **localized, mass-weighted post-impact rebound/spread** metric (upward+outward free-surface momentum or rebound height in the impact neighborhood) that materially exceeds the two-field baseline, **guarded by** a low cap-hit rate (the rebound must not be a clamp artifact), a no-popcorn check (no single-particle ejecta spikes), and conservation (no mass/energy injected). Noisy global upward motion must not satisfy R1.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:43:- **R2 — Real-time:** ≤ 33 ms @ ~200k on the fixed-point i32 grid (no float atomics), under Tint uniformity, within the granted storage-buffer budget (see KD10). The perf premise is "grid is the faster substrate *measured today*" — two-field's 25 ms is a full coupled saturated-pour scene (`docs/PERF_NOTES.md`, `tests/twofield_perf.rs`), whereas XPBD's ~90 ms is extrapolated water-only with levers still open; this justifies *starting* from the grid, not a final elimination of the particle architecture. **Verified by the KD8 prototype + two-velocity cost projection before the full build.**
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:44:- **R3 — Coffee/water coupling at two-field fidelity, conserved** — each sub-claim its own gate, no blanket "fidelity": (a) infiltration/drawdown rate within a plausible band vs the two-field/XPBD reference; (b) saturation field tracks `s = φ_f/n`; (c) **grains seat under a standing water column** (a calibrated no-over-buoyancy gate — the current failure mode, must be measured not assumed resolved); (d) a crater that **holds** under wet-sand plasticity (absolute pit depth persists, per the existing crater-persistence gate); (e) Terzaghi/Skempton effective-stress partition (drained weaker than flooded contrast probe); (f) mass/volume conserved (free + absorbed water, `water_lost==grain_gained`), gate-enforced incl. the long-run saturated tail.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:45:- **R4 — Incompressibility is stiff and density-targeting:** density histogram bounded (ρ/ρ_rest, no over-pack/dense-ring), volume conserved, settled-pool tail KE bounded (no spontaneous stirring), and the pool reflects impacts (the R1 rebound gate). "Reads incompressible" alone is not the gate — the density/volume/tail-KE/rebound quartet is.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:50:- **A — Two-velocity PB-MPM (chosen).** Grid, position-based compliant density constraint (stable stiff push-back at few iterations + a coarse low-frequency pre-pass instead of a full fine Poisson), two momentum fields + `λ`-as-candidate-pore-pressure + Laibe-Price drag + XPBI bed + conserved wetting. Pros: best reconciliation of bounce + measured-today frame-rate + grid-coupling; EA SEED single-phase water reference to anchor the core. Cons/risks: the two-velocity *porous* mixture has no shipped reference (rebuild + research risk); the coarse-grid pre-pass is unbuilt; `λ`-as-pore-pressure is unproven (KD5); perf of the *assembled* system is unproven (KD8 + the two-velocity cost projection retire it); it must beat candidate D on the merits.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:51:- **B — Particle (XPBD) core + grid-assisted coupling (rejected, but not decisively eliminated).** Proven bounce + the coupling is more preserved (XPBD already has pairwise λ-impulse coupling), but XPBD is ~90 ms@200k today and a particle↔grid handoff adds cost/complexity. *Caveat:* the 90 ms is extrapolated water-only with optimization levers explicitly unpulled (`docs/PERF_NOTES.md`, `[[project_perf_target]]`), so it is **not a fair floor** — B loses on *measured-today* perf and on rebuild scope, not on a proven impossibility. Retained as the primary fallback if KD8 fails.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:53:- **D — Compliant predicted-density-error projection on the *existing* two-field operator (rejected, but the closest rival).** The 2026-06-17 brainstorm (`docs/brainstorms/2026-06-17-twofield-water-compliant-density-projection-requirements.md`) proposed a compliant density-error target on the current cell-centered operator — distinct from C (it *does* target density, not `∇·v`) and lower-risk (it preserves the `G·p` cell-centered pressure contract the bed coupling already consumes, and reuses the whole two-field substrate + gates). The reason to prefer full PB-MPM despite D: that doc's compliance-diagonal variant was reviewed as harmful/uncapped-relief-prone, and a projection-on-the-old-operator still inherits the under-converged-Jacobi softness D's own gates struggled with (the DensU thread has ~unresolved gates today). **But this is the candidate the new doc must beat on the merits** — full PB-MPM is justified only if the position-based constraint genuinely bounces where D's projection stays soft, *and* the coupling-rebuild cost (PB-MPM emits position/displacement corrections, while the bed coupling consumes `G·p`) is worth paying. If the KD8 prototype shows PB-MPM doesn't out-bounce a compliant projection, D (cheaper, no coupling rebuild) wins.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:57:**In scope:** a re-architected two-velocity PB-MPM realism core — the position-based density constraint (water incompressibility/bounce), the coarse-grid convergence pre-pass, two momentum fields + Laibe-Price drag, the pore-packing mixture (`ρ₀·(1−φ_s)`, saturation), `λ`-based buoyancy/Terzaghi, conserved wetting/absorption, XPBI bed plasticity/crater, APIC-for-cavity; the KD8 perf prototype; the validation ladder gates.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:59:### Deferred to Follow-Up / Build-Order
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:61:- Surface tension / sub-grid droplets (not required for bounce/liveliness).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:63:**Outside this effort's identity:** the two-field / `main` / XPBD solvers are **not** modified to become the primary — they stay as reference/referee + donors of portable pieces; the just-built transfer work (U1–U3, uncommitted on `feat/twofield-surface-weighted-dissipation`) is superseded (kept as learnings). No **full fine-grid** Poisson is introduced (the point is to avoid the fine RBGS cost); the KD2 coarse pre-pass is global low-frequency pressure work on a coarse level, which is in scope.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:67:- **The KD8 perf prototype is the gating first step** — exact scene/particle count, the bounce metric, the pass bar (≤33 ms@200k), AND the two-velocity cost projection method (stub vs additive estimate). If the projected assembled cost fails, the architecture is reconsidered (fallback: candidate D — compliant projection on the two-field operator, no coupling rebuild; then candidate B; then accept two-field for coupling-only).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:68:- **Does PB-MPM out-bounce candidate D?** The KD8 prototype should include (or be quickly comparable against) a compliant-density-projection variant on the existing two-field operator, so the choice of full PB-MPM over D rests on a measured bounce difference, not assumption.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:69:- **The coarse-grid pressure pre-pass** for PB-MPM 3D convergence: structure, level count, iteration count, and **measured cost** (it is global low-frequency work — its cost must be in the KD8 budget, not hand-waved as "small").
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:71:- **Fixed-point i32 mapping** of the position-based constraint (`λ`, position deltas) on the no-float-atomics grid; FP overflow headroom for *position/displacement* lanes (a new surface — the current `common.wgsl:124` math covers mass/momentum only); storage-buffer ledger (current granted **9**, sanctioned **16**) for two momentum fields + `λ` (ping-pong) + bed state, with a `GpuContext` limit-change gate + live browser verification if >9 (per KD10).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:72:- **Migration shape:** a new solver in `src/solvers/` registered alongside the existing three (the registry pattern), reusing the scene/render/test harness.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:77:- **In-repo empirical record** (this session): two-field **25.4 ms@207k on a full coupled saturated-pour scene** (`docs/PERF_NOTES.md`, `tests/twofield_perf.rs`) but soft/no-bounce; XPBD **~90 ms@200k extrapolated water-only with optimization levers still open** (`docs/PERF_NOTES.md`, `[[project_perf_target]]`) but bounces (the existence proof) and already has pairwise-`max(-λ,0)` coupling + conserved wetting; `main` bounces via a slow RBGS Poisson (40–100 red/black pairs; the volume-projection target at `crates/sim-wasm/src/mpm_3d/shader.rs:765`) + cup blow-up — exact "% of frame" not from a checked-in profiler table, so cited as "pressure-pair loop plausibly dominates" pending the `.deliberate/renders/` profiler artifact. `docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md`.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:82:- **`[[project_solver_redesign_sota]]`** — the prior 11-agent fan-out ranking these candidates (this decision = its candidate A); **`[[project_water_dynamics_unified]]`**, **`[[project_infiltration_ponding]]`**, **`[[project_twofield_cup_compressibility]]`**, **`[[project_perf_target]]`**.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:4:related: docs/brainstorms/2026-06-17-twofield-water-compliant-density-projection-requirements.md
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:7:# Water dynamics: one state-dependent dissipation knob + clean density-target pressure (best of main + XPBD on the two-field base) — requirements
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:15:- **`xpbd`** sets it to a **tuned local** value (XSPH viscosity `xsph_c`, lowered near the free surface so the pour reads as a crown — `water.wgsl:226–267`) and uses a *position-based* density constraint (no velocity-divergence projection to amplify energy) → splashy **and** stable. This is the existence proof for the correct behavior.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:17:The fix is therefore a **state-dependent dissipation field** — preserve momentum for free/airborne/splashing water, dissipate as water merges into the settled pool — keyed on *pool-merging* (depth / local density / relative velocity to the pool), **not** main's `support_ratio` (the specific signal that misfires on drips). Paired with a **clean, density-targeting, energy-non-injecting pressure** so the projection cannot amplify retained energy. Built on the **two-field** base (it has the better, flux-conserving two-velocity coupling), importing main's ballistic-preserve transfer, main's two-sided volume/over-pack projection *target formula*, and XPBD's local-viscosity principle.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:19:This subsumes the earlier narrow "add splash to two-field" framing and the parked over-pack thread: main already ships a two-sided volume+over-pack projection target (`shader.rs:765`) — a proven reference for the DensU work — and the slime, over-pack, and stir all trace to two-field's perf-motivated shortcut (local-Jacobi velocity-PIC + density-relief + flat blend, no momentum preservation), shortcuts main never took. The user has OK'd a real (global) Poisson "if it's fast enough" — main proves a real-time density-targeting sparse-RBGS solve exists in this codebase.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:25:- **`twofield` — over-dissipated everywhere.** `g2p_water` sets `v = Σ w · grid_v` (pure grid-gather, `transfers.wgsl:183`); the only retained particle state is the affine `C`, and `pic_blend = 0.05` (`mod.rs:151`) bleeds even that. The velocity is re-derived entirely from the smoothed, pressure-projected grid every step, so free water forgets its own momentum → goop. Additionally, the density-relief pressure hack injects energy into the settled pool (the two-source stir finding, `tests/twofield_settled.rs:11`), which is why a flat PIC blend was needed at all.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:26:- **`main` — under-dissipated for sparse water, including drips in the cup.** `new_v = mix(grid_v, ballistic_v, preserve)` with `ballistic_v = p.vel + g·dt` and `preserve = clamp((1−support_ratio)·gain·dense_pool_damping, …, cap)` (`shader.rs:2323–2357`). A high-preserve particle largely *ignores* the projected (incompressible) grid velocity. That is correct for genuinely free water but fatal for a slow drip entering the pool: a thin drip has low `support_ratio` → near-max preserve, while `dense_pool_damping` only engages at high local density (which a surface-puncturing drip does not see). The drip keeps its ballistic energy, compresses the incompressible pool, the projection over-corrects next step, FLIP re-injects the energy → accumulating stir / blow-up.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:27:- **`xpbd` — correctly tuned local dissipation.** Explicit XSPH viscosity `Δv = xsph_c·Σ V_w (v_j − v_i) W` (`water.wgsl:226–267`), volume-normalized (resolution-consistent), tuned **lower near the free surface** so the pour impact reads as a crown rather than dissolving. Plus a position-based density constraint (s_corr Δp) — no velocity-divergence projection, so no projection-amplified energy gain. Result: splash + stability.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:31:**Why `main` is not a free base.** main has good water *and* it already solved over-pack — its sparse-RBGS Poisson uses a two-sided volume + explicit over-pack projection target (`volume_projection_target_divergence`, `shader.rs:765`), exactly what the parked DensU thread is rebuilding. But main's water still has the cup blow-up (it needs this same dissipation fix), and its water↔bed coupling is **single grid velocity field** (`shader.rs:57`) with divergence-penalty Darcy infiltration — the kind the team's own analysis (`[[project_infiltration_ponding]]`) deemed fundamentally limited. two-field's flux-conserving two-velocity-field coupling + effective-stress crater is the genuinely better physics and the harder asset to rebuild. So: build on two-field, port main's water + pressure-target ideas in.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:35:- **Build on the two-field base; import the proven mechanisms.** Keep two-field's flux-conserving two-velocity coupling + Terzaghi/Skempton + effective-stress crater. Import: (a) main's state-dependent ballistic/FLIP velocity preservation into `g2p_water`; (b) main's two-sided volume + over-pack projection *target* into the pressure rhs (a proven reference for the parked DensU work); (c) XPBD's local-viscosity principle as the model for the settled-pool dissipation. No second solver, no hybrid handoff.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:36:- **The fix is one state-dependent dissipation field, re-keyed.** Generalize the dissipation from a global constant to a per-particle field: low (ballistic-preserve) for free/airborne/splashing water, high (PIC/XSPH-like) as water merges into the settled pool. **Key it on pool-merging** (submerged depth / local density / relative velocity to the pool), explicitly NOT main's `support_ratio` alone — that is the signal that lets slow drips blow up the cup.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:37:- **Clean, energy-non-injecting pressure so the projection can't amplify retained energy.** Move from the under-converged local-Jacobi + density-relief hack toward a density-targeting, better-converged solve. Use main's volume/over-pack target as the reference formula. A global Poisson (e.g. sparse RBGS on two-field's existing mixture operator) is acceptable **if it holds the real-time budget** — the user's explicit condition. This is what lets the flat PIC blend retire and removes the stir source.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:55:- R7. The two-field bed↔water/crater coupling is unchanged in kind: buoyancy, Terzaghi/Skempton, crater persistence, infiltration/ponding, operator adjoint/SPD gates stay green. The pore pressure remains the cell-centered projection pressure.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:56:- R8. Real-time budget holds (≤ 33 ms @ 200k) on the fixed-point i32 grid (no float atomics), under Tint uniformity (a state-dependent transfer branch must be uniformity-safe), within the storage-buffer budget. The ballistic-preserve term uses the existing per-particle `vel[p]` (common.wgsl:68) — **no new grid buffer**. If a stronger pressure solve (global Poisson / RBGS) is adopted, its cost is measured against the 200k budget and is the gating perf question.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:57:- R9. The over-pack/density work is unified into this effort (not parked-orthogonal): the pressure target is the two-sided volume + over-pack target, with main's `volume_projection_target_divergence` as the reference. ρ/ρ_rest stays bounded (no compaction ring).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:60:- R13. **Coffee grains are not over-buoyant.** In two-field the grains currently float too readily (visual oracle, 2026-06-18). Grain buoyancy is the projection-pressure third-law pair (`Δv_s = −(dt/ρ_s)·gp`, `pressure.wgsl`), so an inflated pore pressure from the over-pack/compressibility would over-buoy the bed — making this plausibly a *symptom* of the same pressure defect R9 addresses. The plan must diagnose the cause (inflated `gp` from over-pack vs grain density ρ_s / effective mass vs drag lift) and ensure settled grains stay seated under a standing water column, without breaking the working buoyancy/Terzaghi partition (R7).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:75:- **A — Unified: state-dependent dissipation + clean density-target pressure on two-field (recommended).** Add main's ballistic/FLIP preserve to `g2p_water`, re-keyed on pool-merging; make the dissipation a per-particle field (free→preserve, pool→dissipate, XSPH/PIC-style); move the pressure toward a density-targeting, better-converged solve using main's target formula. Keeps two-field's coupling. This is the "best of main + xpbd" on the better coupling base.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:76:- **B — Port two-field's coupling onto main (rejected).** main has good water + the over-pack-free density-target Poisson, but rebuilding two-field's two-velocity flux-conserving infiltration + effective-stress crater on main's single-field base is the rewrite in reverse — and main still needs the dissipation fix (cup blow-up). Larger, riskier, throws away the harder asset.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:81:**In scope:** state-dependent dissipation field in the water transfer (preserve free / dissipate pool), re-keyed off pool-merging; the clean density-target pressure (incl. a global/RBGS solve if it fits perf); importing main's target formula + ballistic transfer; the render comparison report; keeping two-field's coupling.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:83:**Deferred (out of scope, may revisit via escalation):** droplets/spray/thin ligaments and surface tension (sub-grid); the full XPBD/grid hybrid (Approach C); grid-resolution increase (perf; the escalation target if the grid floor blocks splash).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:85:**Outside this solver's identity:** XPBD solver logic (not modified — it is a reference); the two-field bed-coupling operator's *kind* (cell-centered pore pressure = projection pressure is preserved).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:89:A prototype of the state-dependent ballistic-preserve transfer was built in `g2p_water` (density-keyed, blend toward `vel[p] + g·dt`, behind a `FLIP_PROTO` toggle) and captured on the render harness (`.deliberate/renders/`, baseline vs prototype vs xpbd):
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:91:- **Win — static/settled water.** The cup pool becomes denser, tighter, and calmer (close to xpbd); the slime/diffuse settled pool is largely fixed by the density-keyed dissipation. Several static-water scenes now read more realistically than baseline.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:92:- **Gap — emitted drop onto the pool still MUSHES.** The transfer change alone does not restore impact splash. This implicates (a) the supporting levers — the `max_speed = 12` cap throttles the crown and the widened jet softens the impact — and (b) the pressure: a too-compressible pool absorbs the impact, so the **density-target pressure (R9) is likely required for the impact to read as a splash**, not the transfer alone. The plan must treat the impact/splash case as transfer + clamp/jet + pressure *together*, not transfer-only.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:93:- **Bug — coffee over-buoyant (R13).** Surfaced in the same review; folded in above as a coupling-realism requirement with the inflated-pore-pressure hypothesis.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:95:These confirm Approach A's direction (the knob works for the settled pool) and sharpen scope: the *impact/splash* half needs the clamp/jet + density-target pressure, not just the transfer.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:99:- **State signal for the dissipation field:** the exact combination (submerged depth, local grid density, relative-velocity-to-pool) and blend-curve shape that preserves splash yet damps slow drips robustly. Resolve empirically against the render scenes (esp. scene 3).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:100:- **Pressure solve choice & perf:** keep local-Jacobi but swap to the two-sided volume/over-pack target, or adopt a global/RBGS Poisson on two-field's mixture operator? The latter best removes the stir source but must be measured at 200k (the user's "if it's fast enough"). main's RBGS is real-time but at an unknown particle count in a single-field context.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:101:- **Interaction of the new pressure target with the two-field mixture coupling:** does main's volume/over-pack target compose cleanly with the pore-pressure=projection-pressure contract (it should — DensU2 already retargeted the rhs)?
