docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:18:Build a new grid-based solver under `src/solvers/` — a two-velocity-field, APIC-transfer, constraint-incompressible multiphase method — that handles all three target interactions in one method: water↔water (incompressible, with impact cavities), granular bed↔itself (Drucker-Prager elastoplasticity, angle of repose, tamping memory), and water↔bed with porosity (Darcy percolation at physical infiltration rates through a deformable, swelling bed, with pour craters). The work is structured as a validation ladder of rungs (L0 water → L1 bed → L2 percolation → L3 full coupling), each rung with quantitative exit gates. This is a pure bet on the novel architecture: a failed gate stops the ladder for redesign — there is no pre-committed fallback path.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:24:Two complete prior attempts failed in complementary halves, and four independent attacks on the pressure solve failed for the same root cause:
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:26:- **main (MPM + global Poisson):** plausible bed deformation, but (a) the pressure solve consumes ~70% of a ~30 ms frame and sparse RBGS (PR #18, 1.93×) is its practical ceiling — Jacobi-CG, adaptive iterations, and indirect dispatch all failed because the real bug is operator inconsistency (compact stride-1 solve vs central stride-2 divergence, A ≠ D·G → a divergence floor no solver beats); (b) water pools above the bed unreasonably long because Darcy resistance is formulated as a divergence penalty in the pressure RHS, which imposes a pressure-jump entry barrier at the bed surface — inconsistent with the correct Stokes–Darcy interface conditions (flux + stress continuity, not pressure continuity); (c) the bed is a fixed scaffold with a scalar porosity — no granular mechanics at all.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:27:- **rewrite (XPBD/PBF):** percolation, wetting/swelling (volume-conserving), and granular statics all work and are test-gated — but position projection derives velocity *after* the constraint solve, so impact momentum dies in the density projection. Both impact-coupling attempts (water→grain committed-dormant; water↔water reverted in `4dca52f`) confirmed there is no contact-pressure layer to patch. No craters, no cavities, no tamping memory.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:37:- R1. Water behaves incompressibly (visual density band per existing water gates) and carries impact momentum: a fast jet into a pool produces a measurable, transiently sustained cavity; a center pour craters a granular bed.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:38:- R2. The bed is a pressure-dependent elastoplastic granular material: tunable angle of repose reaching ≥30° (coffee band 32–48°), column collapse with plausible runout, static rest without creep, and plastic compaction memory under repeated load (tamping persistently raises packing fraction).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:49:- R7. The frame contains no converged global Poisson solve. Incompressibility cost is bounded: one coarse-grid solve (≤ ~1/64 of fine cell count) plus a fixed small number of fine local iterations.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:50:- R8. Dispatches/frame and max-storage-buffers-per-entry-point are first-class budgets, recorded from rung L0 onward via `Solver::profile()`. Each rung also pre-registers a maximum dispatch count and a substep ceiling (with a rough bandwidth estimate) before its implementation starts, so the L3 wall-clock gate cannot be discovered unreachable only after the L1/L2 investment. Sparsity uses over-dispatch + early-out, never `dispatch_workgroups_indirect`.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:51:- R9. Scaling is ~linear in particle count to 200k (ramped: measured at each rung; 200k is the L3 target, not an L0 gate), and real-time is a gated absolute number, not just structure: ≤33 ms/frame median at 200k particles on the reference device (Apple M5) is the L3 exit gate — a perfectly linear 90 ms/frame solver fails the program. Each earlier rung pre-registers a wall-clock ceiling for its representative scene when its gate tests are written; exceeding a ceiling is a halt condition alongside the physics gates. WGSL compiles unmodified for native + web; WebGPU baseline limits except the storage-buffer request.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:61:- KTD-1 — **Two velocity fields per grid node (water + solid), APIC particle↔grid transfers.** Percolation requires co-located phases at *different* velocities (relative Darcy flux); contact requires momentum exchange on a shared grid. One field per phase satisfies both; velocity-averaging couplers (Genesis) are structurally rejected. APIC (per-particle affine matrix C) is the momentum carrier that makes cavities — its absence is precisely why XPBD craters failed. Per the v1 data-path lesson (`docs/issues/010-separate-water-bed-data-paths.md`), water and solid particles occupy separate buffer ranges with phase-specific dispatches, not a shared branchy buffer.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:62:- KTD-2 — **Incompressibility = coarse-grid pressure seed + fine local Jacobi cleanup, on one discretely consistent operator family.** The coarse solve (e.g. 1/4³ resolution) kills the low-frequency modes local iteration can't reach (this is the unbuilt PB-MPM 3D-convergence fix — the novel core); a fixed budget of fine Jacobi sweeps kills high-frequency residual. No converged Poisson (main's graveyard), no EOS-only fallback for the shipped path (an adaptive-Tait weak-compressibility predictor, c_s ≈ 10·v_max, is permitted as a pre-projection stabilizer if implementation finds it helps). **Operator consistency is a stated gate, not an aspiration:** divergence, gradient, and both grids' Laplacians come from one MAC-consistent family, the coarse operators are consistent restrictions of the fine ones, and rung L0 must measure the post-projection divergence and show it decays below a pre-registered tolerance as the iteration budget grows — a floor that plateaus above tolerance is the failure, and it is the exact signature that killed main's adaptive-RBGS, so it must be demonstrably absent. Two-field corollary from production Euler-Euler practice (the multiphase Rhie-Chow lesson, Cubero 2007 / Ferreira 2021): whatever velocity feeds the divergence must carry the drag and solids-pressure contributions consistently with the momentum update, or φ_s develops spurious oscillations even when the pressure operators themselves are consistent.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:63:- KTD-3 — **Darcy drag is a velocity body force folded semi-implicitly into the grid update — never a divergence penalty.** Form: drag rate β(φ) from `src/models/permeability.rs` (Kozeny–Carman), applied per node between the two velocity fields via the analytic exponential (Laibe–Price) update `Δv⁺ = Δv·exp(−β·dt)` — exact for frozen-coefficient linear drag at any stiffness (β refreshes per substep; when β varies in-step through φ, slip, or Re the update is formally first-order, so the honest claim is robust stability at coffee's worst-case density ratio ρ_f/ρ_s ≈ 0.83, not unconditional exactness), momentum-conserving by construction (mass-weighted pair update). Main's divergence-penalty formulation is recorded as a named anti-pattern: it blocks interface entry flux and is the root cause of the stuck water layer. This per-node analytic fold is the same structure production Euler-Euler codes use for stiff interphase drag (Fluent PC-SIMPLE's Partial Elimination Algorithm; MFiX-Exa reports drag iteration counts dropping from 50+ to 3–5 with it) — a 2×2 closed-form inversion, zero global communication. Two production refinements are adopted: (1) **drag-law blending** — Kozeny-Carman/Ergun is the packed-bed law and over-predicts drag in dilute regions (pour entry, splashed grains); blend toward a dilute-suspension law (Wen-Yu form) below φ_s ≈ 0.2 with a smooth transition (the Gidaspow/Huilin-Gidaspow pattern every TFM code ships); (2) if residual added-mass jitter appears despite the exponential fold, the standard stabilizer is a **virtual-mass term** (C_vm ≈ 0.5) added symmetrically to both phases — a named lever, default off.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:64:- KTD-4 — **Infiltration interface physics: mixture incompressibility + wetting-front suction + phase-change absorption.** (a) Continuity is per-phase volume conservation, `∂φ_k/∂t + ∇·(φ_k·v_k) = s_k`, where the s_k carry the absorption/swelling/compaction exchanges; summing gives the projection constraint `∇·(φ_f·v_f + φ_s·v_s) = s_net`, and the projection RHS carries s_net explicitly rather than assuming zero (s_net = 0 only when the exchanges are volume-balanced, which R4 mandates and the conservation gates verify). The momentum side is pinned as equations, not prose: `φ_k·ρ_k·Dv_k/Dt = −φ_k·∇p + ∇·(φ_k·τ_k) + f_drag,k + φ_k·ρ_k·g` (Gao 2018's locally-averaged form), so the intrinsic-velocity pressure correction is `Δv_k = −(Δt/ρ_k)·∇p` — the φ_k cancels; it does NOT scale the velocity update. Pinned invariants: a skeleton at rest under hydrostatic pressure stays at rest (in the rigid-skeleton regime the solid field is not projected into motion — pressure enters the bed as buoyancy and effective stress, i.e., a reaction); submerged grains feel buoyancy through the momentum equation (not just constitutively); a compacting or swelling skeleton displaces pore water instead of silently creating or destroying volume. (b) A capillary suction body force acts on water at the unsaturated wetting front (Green–Ampt; ψ_f ≈ 50–110 mm physical for ~400 µm media, mapped to reduced units), gated by a hydrophobic→wetted transition so the bloom-phase entry delay falls out naturally. (c) Absorption is a moisture phase-change at contact (GIC-style): grid-projected moisture deficit drives transfer from water particles into per-grain moisture, reusing the existing volume-conserving wetting law (`src/models/wetting.rs`) — absorption starts on contact, not after interpenetration.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:65:- KTD-5 — **Bed constitutive model: Klar et al. 2016 Drucker-Prager return map** (3-branch, log-strain, one 3×3 SVD per solid particle per substep) with saturation-dependent cohesion from `src/models/cohesion.rs` (Bishop-style effective stress) and a compaction cap surface for tamping memory (plastic volumetric strain persists and feeds packing fraction → permeability). The tensile-apex branch is what lets crater walls stand and then collapse. **Stress partition is stated once and audited:** total stress = effective stress (DP + cap, with the U5 over-packing solids-pressure guard living INSIDE the contact/effective partition) + pore pressure, and the projection pressure IS the pore pressure — no volumetric mode may be resisted twice by contact pressure, cap pressure, solids-pressure guard, and projection pressure stacking up. The U7 stress-partition audit gates this.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:66:- KTD-6 — **Cavity sustain via constraint bubbles** (Goldade & Batty 2017): free-surface cells are tracked; enclosed air pockets get one aggregate volume constraint each in the pressure solve, so the pour cavity doesn't instantly re-level. Rung L0 ships the free-surface treatment plus at minimum the single-bubble case (the pour cavity is one pocket); generalization to arbitrary pocket counts may defer.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:67:- KTD-7 — **GPU layout discipline:** P2G scatter uses fixed-point `atomic<i32>` accumulation (KEEP.md §3 pattern, FP_SCALE re-validated against the velocity cap with documented overflow headroom); bind groups are split by pass family (transfers / pressure / plasticity / coupling) per `docs/issues/003-split-mpm-bind-groups.md`; every entry point fits the device's storage-buffer request. **Discrepancy to resolve in U1:** `src/utils/gpu.rs` currently requests exactly 9 storage buffers (`NEEDED_STORAGE_BUFFERS`) while AGENTS.md sanctions 16 — the scaffold unit raises the request only if the two-field grid genuinely needs it and verifies browser grants; grid fields are packed into vec4 lanes first.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:68:- KTD-8 — **Porosity and permeability are local, evolving fields:** φ_s from solid-phase P2G mass (smooth via the transfer kernel), K(φ) per node via Kozeny–Carman, updated by swelling, plastic compaction, and (later, deferred) fines. A configured global porosity scalar — main's approach — is rejected; measured intra-bed K heterogeneity spans an order of magnitude.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:69:- KTD-9 — **The validation ladder is the project spine and the execution posture.** Each rung's gate tests are written before that rung's physics (test-first at the gate level, mirroring the analytic-test doctrine: discrete-recurrence references, CPU twins of GPU kernels, set-invariant assertions, headless GPU gating, no `#[ignore]`). A failed gate halts the ladder for redesign — by explicit decision there is no pre-committed fallback architecture. To make the halt implementable rather than a tuning purgatory, each rung declares a knob grid when its gate tests are written, and the grid must enumerate EVERY tunable its gates depend on — for U3: coarse ratio {1/4³, 1/8³} × fine-sweep range × Tait predictor on/off × pressure tolerance; later rungs add timestep/substep cap, suction parameters, drag blend width, solids-pressure stiffness, plastic damping, and boundary/filter coefficients as applicable; exhausting the grid without a pass IS the halt condition, and reaching for any knob outside the grid is a redesign decision, not a retry.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:70:- KTD-10 — **Exposure:** native examples and the registry first; the web frontend (`src/web.rs` hard-codes `XpbdSolver`) is not generalized in this plan — deferred to follow-up work once L3 passes.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:84:  D --> E[Coarse-grid pressure solve<br/>restricted operators, bubble constraints]
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:94:  L0[L0 water: APIC + coarse-seed incompressibility + cavity<br/>GATES: divergence decays below pre-registered tolerance,<br/>momentum conserved, jet cavity forms, budgets recorded]
