docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:103:**Calibration targets for L2/L3 gates** (from the brewing literature; map to reduced units via KEEP.md §2, ≈27.7 sim-units/m, 5.20 mL/sim-unit³):
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:125:- **Goal:** A buildable, registered, inert two-field solver skeleton that satisfies the seam contract and pins the GPU budgets.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:129:- **Approach:** Mirror the xpbd module shape: one Pod `Params` byte-mirrored in WGSL with a size assert; auto-layout pipelines via `make`/`bg` closures; stable global binding indices; all passes through a `dispatch_pass`-style helper feeding `Profile`. Separate water/solid particle ranges in the canonical `ParticleBuffers` layout so `ui::Renderer` works unchanged. Resolve the 9-vs-16 storage-buffer discrepancy here: pack grid fields into vec4 lanes first, raise `NEEDED_STORAGE_BUFFERS` only if required, and verify a web build still gets the device.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:140:- **Approach:** Quadratic B-spline weights (KEEP.md §3 validated math), fixed-point `atomic<i32>` P2G scatter with documented FP_SCALE/velocity-cap headroom, per-particle affine C matrix carried and updated in G2P. Gravity on the grid, plus grid-node solid-boundary treatment against the scene SDF solids (separating/no-penetration condition on grid velocities, mirroring `src/utils/sdf.rs` conventions with a "mirrors utils::sdf" WGSL comment) — boundary handling is scoped work here, not an afterthought. No pressure yet — this unit's water is a momentum-correct splashing dust.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:143:- **Test scenarios:** free-fall matches the solver's own discrete recurrence within a documented float budget; P2G→G2P round trip conserves total mass and linear momentum exactly (fixed-point) for random particle sets; angular-momentum improvement of APIC vs PIC demonstrated on a rotating blob (discrimination gap asserted); fixed-point overflow probe at the velocity cap; particles settle on the container floor without wall penetration (grid boundary-condition gate); no NaNs across 1000 steps of a dam-break-shaped fall.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:148:- **Goal:** Water is visually incompressible at bounded, Poisson-free cost — and the operator-inconsistency failure mode is demonstrably absent.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:151:- **Files:** create `src/solvers/twofield/pressure.wgsl`; modify `mod.rs`, `common.wgsl`; test `tests/twofield_pressure.rs`.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:152:- **Approach:** MAC-consistent divergence/gradient/Laplacian family on the fine grid; coarse grid (ratio an implementation choice, start 1/4³) with operators built as consistent restrictions (Galerkin or rediscretized-with-verification); coarse solve (few cheap sweeps on ≤ ~11k cells), prolongate as pressure seed, then a fixed budget of fine Jacobi sweeps and projection of the porosity-weighted divergence (φ_f = 1 everywhere in this unit). Solid-cell Neumann pressure conditions at walls are part of the MAC-consistent operator family — boundary stencils are inside the consistency requirement, not bolted on. Optional adaptive-Tait predictor behind an opt-in gate defaulting off. Sparse execution via over-dispatch + early-out on empty tiles.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:154:- **Patterns to follow:** residual-adaptive Status-struct pattern from xpbd (GPU-side convergence state, no per-iteration readback); `docs/issues/002-pressure-solve-cost.md` observability recommendations.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:155:- **Test scenarios:** operator unit gates run BEFORE any fluid test (the production MMS/code-verification pattern, Salari-Knupp): discrete adjointness `u·(Gp) = −(Du)·p` to machine precision for random fields on the staggered layout, the same check for the coarse restriction/prolongation pair, a manufactured-solution order-of-accuracy sweep on D/G/L (observed log-log error slope matches the formal order), and — decisively — the same checks on the ASSEMBLED projection operator `A = −D·M⁻¹·G` exactly as used in the solve (mass/φ weighting, solid masks, boundary rows included), at both fine and coarse-with-transfer levels, on masked domains representative of real scenes (closed tank, sloped wall, V60 cone, partially filled bed) — a self-consistent D/G pair must not be able to pass while the seeded solve injects pressure through a different operator, and boundary-adjacent restriction/prolongation is exactly where multigrid-like schemes break, so box-only tests are insufficient; a two-grid convergence factor measured on known low- and high-frequency modes of those masked domains; then post-projection RMS divergence — measured with the projection's own divergence operator, interior cells only (surface cells are gated in U4) — drops by at least a stated factor (≥2×) per doubling of the fine-sweep budget until it falls below a pre-registered absolute tolerance, where the tolerance is derived from the visible-error budget (the xpbd density-band envelope) and the sweep count needed to reach it must fit the rung's pre-registered wall-clock ceiling (meeting tolerance only at non-real-time sweep counts fails L0 now, not L3 later); because the self-measured divergence gate is gameable, independent volume gates run alongside it: particle-density drift band, tank water-height drift, zero net flux through closed walls, free-surface fill-fraction conservation; a floor that plateaus above tolerance FAILS the gate (monotonic-but-plateauing was exactly main's A≠D·G signature); run on both a static tank and mid-splash dam break; A/B coarse-seed-on vs off shows the seed removes low-frequency residual (tank pressure profile approaches hydrostatic); with the adaptive-Tait predictor enabled, an A/B run must show a measurable fine-iteration-budget reduction at equal divergence tolerance — below threshold the predictor is removed rather than shipped dormant; density band on settled dam break matches the existing xpbd water-gate envelope; cost gate — dispatches/frame within the recorded U1 budget + a stated increment; long-run (≥2000 steps) settled tank stays settled (no pressure-driven creep or popcorn).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:163:- **Files:** create `src/solvers/twofield/surface.wgsl`; modify `pressure.wgsl`, `mod.rs`; test `tests/twofield_cavity.rs`.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:164:- **Approach:** Free-surface cell classification (fill fraction from P2G mass) with surface Dirichlet handling in the pressure solve; constraint-bubble volume constraint for the enclosed pour pocket. The bubble constraint's representation is pinned, not hand-waved: one explicit Lagrange-multiplier scalar per pocket (its value IS the pocket pressure), coupled to the pocket-boundary pressure faces, and represented IDENTICALLY in the coarse solve and the fine cleanup — a coarse-only row that the fine Jacobi sweeps relax away is the named failure mode. Pour emission reuses `src/emission/`.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:172:- **Goal:** The bed is a real granular material on the solid velocity field: elastic stress, pressure-dependent yield, plastic compaction memory.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:176:- **Approach:** Per-solid-particle deformation gradient F (or elastic strain in log space), 3×3 SVD in WGSL (port a verified branch-free routine; CPU twin mandatory), Klar 3-branch return map (elastic / yield-surface projection / tensile apex), friction angle from `Materials`, cohesion from `src/models/cohesion.rs` as a function of local saturation (dry in this rung), volumetric cap surface whose consolidation pressure ratchets with plastic compaction → packing fraction state per particle. Grid-level over-packing guard (the TFM solids-pressure pattern): φ_s is hard-clipped at the packing limit and a solids-pressure term that diverges as φ_s → φ_max resists further compaction in the two-field update — the particle-level return map alone cannot guarantee this on the grid. The guard enters the solid momentum equation as part of the effective/contact stress per KTD-5's partition — it is NOT a separate volume constraint and never appears in the projection; the U7 stress-partition audit verifies it is counted exactly once. Cost bound: solid counts are ~10–15k grains against 100k+ water, so the SVD/return-map is bounded; demoting it from per-substep to per-frame is a permitted knob ONLY if the repose/collapse gates are shown insensitive to the choice.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:188:- **Dependencies:** U3, plus a thin slice of U2's solid-phase P2G (static seeded grains contributing mass to φ_s only — no stress, no return map). Deliberately NOT dependent on U5: the rigid-skeleton regime needs no plasticity, so the program's second-riskiest novelty (two-field drag + mixture projection) is gated immediately after rung L0, in parallel with U5, before the plasticity investment is sunk. Scope caveat: U6 validates the two-field formulation only in the frozen-bed limit — moving φ_s, compaction-driven pore pressure, and effective-stress feedback are gated in U7, and U6 passing does not certify them.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:189:- **Files:** create `src/solvers/twofield/coupling.wgsl`; modify `pressure.wgsl` (porosity-weighted divergence), `mod.rs`; test `tests/twofield_coupling.rs`.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:190:- **Approach:** φ_s per node from solid P2G mass; β(φ) via `src/models/permeability.rs` mirrored in WGSL ("mirrors models::" comment convention); Laibe–Price exponential drag pair update between the two node velocities (mass-weighted, momentum-conserving); the mixture constraint ∇·(φ_f·v_f + φ_s·v_s) = 0 (with v_s = 0 while the skeleton is frozen) replaces plain divergence in the projection, with the intrinsic-velocity correction Δv_k = −(Δt/ρ_k)·∇p exactly per KTD-4 — φ weights the continuity/operator mass terms, never the velocity correction itself. Column-outflow boundary at the container base for the drained-flux tests. The bed is kinematically frozen in this unit (rigid-skeleton regime) so drag and pressure formulation are isolated from plasticity.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:193:- **Test scenarios:** drained flux through a static saturated column scales ∝ K(φ)·head and ∝ d² across a grind sweep (ordering + ratio bands); water entering the bed surface sees no pressure-jump barrier — entry flux matches the head-driven Darcy prediction within a stated band (the anti-divergence-penalty gate); drag pair update conserves combined momentum per node exactly; a settled saturated column stays settled long-run (drag does not pump energy); a submerged dense block in hydrostatic water exerts/receives the correct buoyant reaction — measured as the reaction impulse on the CONSTRAINED (frozen) solid equal to displaced-volume weight, since a frozen body has no dynamics to observe (dynamic buoyant motion is gated in U7); an undrained vertical load on a sealed saturated column transfers fully to pore pressure via the boundary reaction (the quantitative Skempton-B gate lives in U7 — a kinematically frozen skeleton has no volumetric strain path, so B is not meaningful here); the drag↔projection split is gated as a coupled system rather than assumed stable from the fold alone: under timestep refinement, slip-velocity decay matches the analytic exponential within a band with no oscillation growth and no energy increase (the partitioned-coupling gate at ρ_f/ρ_s ≈ 0.83); β-variation stress tests — high slip (nonlinear regime), dry→wet transition, φ near packing — show bounded error against a subcycled reference, where the reference subcycles drag AND projection together on a closed variable-φ two-phase column with outflow (the hard case is the full coupled loop, not drag decay in isolation); face-velocity consistency check — the velocities feeding D(φv) carry the same drag, pressure, solids-pressure, and boundary contributions as the momentum update (the multiphase Rhie-Chow corollary from KTD-2; a manufactured field where omitting any one term produces a detectable spurious φ_s oscillation); the U3 divergence-decay gate re-runs on a variable-φ saturated column against the mixture divergence (operator consistency in the φ<1 regime — the regime the novelty claim lives in); free-water region (φ_f = 1) behavior is byte-identical to U3 results (coupling terms vanish where there is no bed).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:194:- **Verification:** Darcy-scaling and entry-flux gates green; anti-pattern check — no Darcy term anywhere in the pressure RHS.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:202:- **Approach:** Wetting-front suction body force at unsaturated-front nodes (Green–Ampt magnitude from the calibration table, mapped to reduced units), gated by per-grain wetted state (hydrophobic→wetted transition with a time/contact threshold → the bloom delay); GIC-style moisture phase-change using `src/models/wetting.rs` demand curves (volume 1:1 into grain moisture; effective mass and diameter swell per the existing law); swelling and compaction feed the local φ/K fields from U6. Phase-selective boundary at the V60 filter surface: the solid field sees a wall, the water field sees open outflow per the filter SDF species mask — the pond-drain gate cannot pass without it. This is exactly Fluent's porous-jump membrane condition (Δp = (μ/α·v_n + C₂·½ρv_n²)·Δm applied to the water field's normal flux; wall for the solid field), so the filter's flow resistance is a calibrated Darcy+Forchheimer face coefficient, not ad hoc. If the wetting-front suction nonlinearity destabilizes the explicit update at the dry limit, the named remedy is L-scheme stabilization (add L·(s_new − s_old) with L ≥ half the retention curve's Lipschitz constant — unconditionally convergent exactly where Newton-style updates fail).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:205:- **Test scenarios:** infiltration into a dry wettable bed starts on contact (no interpenetration delay) and early-time rate exceeds the bare-Darcy rate when suction is enabled (Green–Ampt regime A/B); with the bloom gate enabled, a dry bed shows a seconds-scale entry delay then accelerates (two-regime discrimination); pour at demand > capacity ponds, pond depth stabilizes, and after pour stop the pond drains within the calibrated band (medium-grind scenario mapped to reduced units); total volume (free + absorbed + pore) conserved exactly over the run including a ≥2000-step saturated tail; absorption conserves momentum under the effective-mass convention; a manufactured source-term test pins the s_net plumbing — prescribed local s_f/s_s in a closed saturated bed must produce the compensating pore-water flux and pressure response through the projection RHS with no density drift (total-volume conservation alone cannot catch a projection that ignores the sources); grains swell to the calibrated magnitude and the local K drops accordingly (monotonic gate); water drains through the filter boundary while grains are retained (phase-selective boundary gate).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:216:- **Approach:** Release the solid field under saturated conditions: pore pressure feeds effective stress (Bishop saturation weighting) into the return map; wet cohesion from the existing curve; impact momentum arrives through the shared-grid drag/contact path (no pairwise impulse bolt-ons — the named failed approach). Crater metric = center surface depression vs rim, against the swelling baseline.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:217:- **Test scenarios:** center pour produces a crater whose depth exceeds the swelling lift (signed metric > 0, vs the documented main/xpbd baselines of ≈0); crater walls slump when the jet stops (tensile-apex behavior, no frozen spikes); saturated bed under sustained pour does not fluidize at calibrated drag (skeleton holds, per the xpbd gate); the Terzaghi 1D consolidation benchmark gates the pore-pressure/effective-stress coupling — sudden load on a saturated column, pore-pressure dissipation U(T) matches the analytic series solution within a stated band, run at TWO drainage lengths so the T = c_v·t/H² collapse verifies the scaling rather than one tuned curve, with the load-sharing check that effective stress + pore pressure sum to the applied total load throughout, and the quantitative Skempton-B gate (undrained pore-pressure response ratio ≈ 1 for incompressible constituents) measured here on the DEFORMABLE skeleton — U6's frozen skeleton has no volumetric strain path, so this is the first unit where B is meaningful (the standard geotech u-p validation; note the Vermeer-Verruijt caveat that too-small drainage steps produce spurious pore-pressure oscillation); stress-partition audit — in a static saturated column, total vertical stress equals effective stress + pore pressure within tolerance (no volumetric mode resisted twice across contact, cap, solids-pressure guard, and projection pressure, per KTD-5); per-frame impulse ledger — pressure, drag, gravity, and boundary/filter reaction impulses account for each phase's momentum change separately, with open-boundary (filter outflow) fluxes tracked explicitly; combined long-run conservation (volume incl. swelling, momentum) on the full V60 pour scene; the L2 ponding/drain bands re-run on the deformable bed (swelling and plastic compaction shift the K field the bands were gated under); a reduced divergence-decay check re-runs with the deformable bed; determinism — two seeded runs produce identical metrics.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:222:- **Goal:** Demonstrate the perf thesis (no Poisson → headroom) and finish the seam: scaling to 200k, budgets vs xpbd, metrics surface, docs.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:226:- **Approach:** Scaling probe across 5k→200k on water-only and full scenes; per-pass timestamp breakdown via `profile()`; compare against the xpbd baseline (profiler-as-referee, recorded in PERF_NOTES); populate `Metrics` (drawdown time, evenness placeholders where extraction is deferred); ensure the test readback surface matches the xpbd precedent so future suites can port.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:227:- **Test scenarios:** µs/Kparticle flat within a stated factor from 40k→200k (linearity gate; the maximum acceptable degradation factor is pre-registered before the 200k run, not derived from it); dispatches/frame within the final recorded budget; full V60 scene at 200k runs ≤33 ms/frame median on the reference device (the R9 real-time gate), with the gate scene's composition pre-registered before the run — particle mix (~185–190k water + 10–15k solids per KTD-5's cost bound), grid resolution, substep count, and scene duration are fixed in the test, so the gate cannot be passed with a conveniently cheap mix; an offline grid-refinement sweep (3 resolutions, 2:1 ratio) with Richardson extrapolation/GCI on one fixed scenario — for this offline study the fine-sweep budget SCALES with grid resolution so iterative error stays subdominant to truncation error (a fixed absolute sweep count would fail the asymptotic-range check for reasons unrelated to spatial discretization; the fixed real-time budget is gated separately by U3's tolerance); extrapolated values become stored regression bands; `metrics()`/`particles()`/`profile()` complete without GPU sync (timing-based smoke); web build compiles (`wasm32` target check).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:234:**In scope:** everything above — the three interactions, conservation gates, calibration bands, 200k scaling demonstration, registry integration, native-example exposure.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:236:**Deferred to follow-up work:**
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:243:- Lode-angle-dependent yield surface (e.g. Matsuoka–Nakai) to remove the grid-strength component of measured repose — the U5 grid-resolution confound documented in `tests/twofield_bed.rs` (h = 1.5 arm 29.9° vs h = 2 base 44.3°).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:251:- **Coarse-seed convergence is the novel bet (highest risk).** The PB-MPM 3D-convergence fix this implements has no published reference implementation. Mitigation: it is the *first* gated unit after transfers (U3), the gate is quantitative (divergence-floor curve), and by explicit decision a gate failure halts for redesign rather than silently shipping a compressible-looking fluid. Watch specifically for coarse/fine operator inconsistency reproducing the A≠D·G floor — the gate is designed to catch exactly this.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:252:- **Added-mass instability at ρ_f/ρ_s ≈ 0.83.** Explicit drag coupling diverges at near-unity density ratios; the Laibe–Price exponential fold is the mitigation (exact for linear drag at any stiffness). If the drag↔projection Lie split still shows coupling artifacts, the escalation ladder is named and bounded: Strang splitting (drag/2 → project → drag/2, O(dt²) splitting error at ~2× cost), then 2–5 Aitken-relaxed sub-iterations of the pair (the partitioned-FSI standard), then the virtual-mass term from KTD-3 — all short of redesign. Residual risk: nonlinear (Forchheimer) corrections at high flux, deferred unless gates demand them.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:253:- **Storage-buffer ceiling.** Two grid velocity fields + mass + pressure + divergence + φ + flags is buffer-hungry; the device currently grants 9/stage. Mitigation in U1: vec4 packing first, raise the request second, verify web. If a pass genuinely cannot fit, split it (dispatch budget absorbs it — that's what the budget gate is for).
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:255:- **Fixed-point P2G contention/overflow.** Known-hot in dense regions (`docs/issues/004-reduce-p2g-atomic-contention.md`); FP_SCALE headroom is re-validated in U2 and coupled to the velocity cap. Workgroup-local accumulation is the named lever if contention shows up in the per-pass timings.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:256:- **Calibration-unit mapping.** The physical bands (K, ψ_f, drain times) must be mapped into reduced units (KEEP.md §2) before gates freeze; KEEP warns the length scale is baked into permeability timescales. The mapping is an explicit early task inside U6, not an afterthought.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:261:## Deferred to Implementation
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:264:- Whether the adaptive-Tait predictor stays (it ships behind an opt-in gate defaulting off; keep only if U3/U4 show it reduces iteration budget).
