docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:9:# Re-architect the realism core to two-velocity-field PB-MPM (stiff grid, no Poisson) — requirements
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:13:The coffee-pour water must **bounce/scatter on impact** and read as real water, while keeping the two assets the current two-field solver has — the **coffee/water coupling** and the **frame rate** (real-time, ~200k particles / ≤33 ms). A long patching campaign on the two-field grid solver (surface-weighted dissipation transfer, density-relief/density-target pressure, merge discriminator) did **not** deliver realistic water. The converged diagnosis: **a bounce is a stiff-incompressibility (pressure) response — the pool must spike pressure and shove the impacting water back** — and the two-field's **under-converged velocity-divergence (`∇·v`) Jacobi projection is fundamentally soft** (it's fast *because* it under-converges, which is the same reason it can't bounce). A fully-converged grid Poisson would bounce but is slow (`main`'s sparse-RBGS Poisson runs 40–100 red/black pressure pairs and its pressure-pair loop plausibly dominates the frame — exact share pending a checked-in profiler artifact, see Sources).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:15:**Decision: re-architect the realism core to two-velocity-field PB-MPM** (Position-Based MPM — the SOTA fan-out's candidate **A**). PB-MPM keeps everything **on the grid** (the faster path measured here today: two-field runs 25 ms@207k on a full coupled scene vs the XPBD particle core's ~90 ms@200k *extrapolated water-only with optimization levers still open* — see R2/KD8 for why this proves "grid is faster today," not that particle XPBD is eliminated) and replaces the soft `∇·v` projection with a **compliant, position-based density constraint** (the XPBD/PBF mechanism). The bet is that this gives **stable local stiffness at a few iterations — enough for a visible bounce/push-back — without a full fine-grid Poisson**. It does **not** claim to deliver globally-converged stiff incompressibility for free: low-frequency density information still has to propagate across the domain, which is why KD2 carries an explicit **coarse-grid pressure pre-pass** (an approximate global low-frequency solve — cheaper than a full Poisson, but global pressure work nonetheless, to be measured not assumed). XPBD is the in-repo existence proof that few-iteration compliant position constraints bounce in real time. The coffee/water **coupling is rebuilt** on this substrate: two co-located momentum fields, a pore-packing density target, Laibe-Price drag, XPBI bed plasticity, and conserved absorption — patterns proven in pieces across XPBD and the two-field. Whether the constraint multiplier `λ` can be converted into a usable pore-pressure field for buoyancy/Terzaghi is a **hypothesis to validate (KD5)**, not a settled mechanism. The assembled two-velocity *porous* PB-MPM has no shipped reference (EA SEED's PB-MPM is single-phase water); that novelty is a **risk to retire by building**, separate from the validated repo physics it ports.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:17:This supersedes the two-field-patching path; the two-field, `main` MPM, and XPBD become reference/referee solvers (and donors of portable pieces + the validation harness).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:21:- **Bounce = stiff incompressibility.** Water bounces/scatters off the cup/pool only if the fluid is stiff enough that an impact spikes pressure and reflects the incoming momentum. XPBD and `main` MPM bounce; two-field does not — it "just pools where it is" (visual oracle). Empirically, every two-field water failure (mushy/no-bounce, over-pack, over-buoyant coffee, settled stirring) traces to its `∇·v` projection: it's under-converged (residual compressibility), it doesn't even constrain density, and the projection dissipates the impact momentum that should bounce.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:22:- **The perf↔stiffness trap on the classical grid.** Converge the Poisson → stiff but slow; under-converge → fast but soft. Two-field chose fast/soft. `main` chose a converged-ish RBGS Poisson (40–100 red/black pressure pairs; the pressure-pair loop plausibly dominates its frame — soften pending a checked-in profiler artifact, see Sources) with an operator-inconsistency divergence floor. Neither gives bounce **and** speed.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:23:- **PB-MPM relaxes (not escapes) the trap.** It replaces the soft `∇·v` projection with a **compliant position-based density constraint** (XPBD-style): the compliance gives a *stable, stiff restoring force* on density error at a few iterations (→ local push-back → visible bounce) without converging a fine-grid solve. This buys **stable local stiffness**, not globally-converged incompressibility — the EA PB-MPM work itself notes Jacobi-style parallel constraint solving converges slowly / stays artificially soft, and presents *stability at any stiffness*, not exact stiffness, as the win. Globally-stiff 3D incompressibility still needs low-frequency information to cross the domain; KD2's coarse-grid pre-pass supplies that as **approximate global low-frequency pressure work** (cheaper than a full fine Poisson, but not free, and to be measured). The net perf claim is that this is cheaper than `main`'s fine RBGS Poisson, *not* that global pressure work disappears. EA SEED ships the single-phase core real-time in WebGPU.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:29:- **KD1 — Position-based compliant density constraint replaces the `∇·v` projection.** It targets **density** (J / volume) directly, so it cannot suffer the two-field's density-blind over-pack, and the compliance gives a *stable* stiff restoring force at a few P2G→solve→G2P iterations. Expected outcome: stable local stiffness sufficient for a visible bounce/push-back — **not** a guarantee of globally-converged stiff incompressibility at that iteration budget (a parallel Jacobi-style constraint solve converges slowly across the domain; bounce is a local response, global incompressibility is not). Globally-stiff behavior is reconciled with speed by KD2's coarse pre-pass, not by the constraint alone. (Existence proof for the *local* claim: XPBD/PBF bounces real-time today with this mechanism.)
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:30:- **KD2 — Stay on the grid; port EA SEED's WebGPU PB-MPM as the single-phase water reference, plus a coarse-grid pressure pre-pass.** Honors the frame-rate asset and gives a concrete reference for the water core. The pre-pass is an **approximate global low-frequency pressure solve** (a small multigrid-ish / coarse-level helper) — it is global pressure work, just on a coarse level, so the perf claim is "cheaper than a full fine-grid Poisson," not "no global solve." Its structure, level count, iteration count, and measured cost are an open planning question (see Outstanding Questions). This is the one substantively "unbuilt piece."
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:46:- **R5 — The architecture is a coherent whole** (one solver, the three interactions coupled), not a patchwork; the two-field/main/xpbd remain as reference/referee solvers and as the source of portable pieces (XPBI, conserved wetting, Laibe-Price drag, the render harness).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:50:- **A — Two-velocity PB-MPM (chosen).** Grid, position-based compliant density constraint (stable stiff push-back at few iterations + a coarse low-frequency pre-pass instead of a full fine Poisson), two momentum fields + `λ`-as-candidate-pore-pressure + Laibe-Price drag + XPBI bed + conserved wetting. Pros: best reconciliation of bounce + measured-today frame-rate + grid-coupling; EA SEED single-phase water reference to anchor the core. Cons/risks: the two-velocity *porous* mixture has no shipped reference (rebuild + research risk); the coarse-grid pre-pass is unbuilt; `λ`-as-pore-pressure is unproven (KD5); perf of the *assembled* system is unproven (KD8 + the two-velocity cost projection retire it); it must beat candidate D on the merits.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:63:**Outside this effort's identity:** the two-field / `main` / XPBD solvers are **not** modified to become the primary — they stay as reference/referee + donors of portable pieces; the just-built transfer work (U1–U3, uncommitted on `feat/twofield-surface-weighted-dissipation`) is superseded (kept as learnings). No **full fine-grid** Poisson is introduced (the point is to avoid the fine RBGS cost); the KD2 coarse pre-pass is global low-frequency pressure work on a coarse level, which is in scope.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-19-pbmpm-rearchitecture-requirements.md:77:- **In-repo empirical record** (this session): two-field **25.4 ms@207k on a full coupled saturated-pour scene** (`docs/PERF_NOTES.md`, `tests/twofield_perf.rs`) but soft/no-bounce; XPBD **~90 ms@200k extrapolated water-only with optimization levers still open** (`docs/PERF_NOTES.md`, `[[project_perf_target]]`) but bounces (the existence proof) and already has pairwise-`max(-λ,0)` coupling + conserved wetting; `main` bounces via a slow RBGS Poisson (40–100 red/black pairs; the volume-projection target at `crates/sim-wasm/src/mpm_3d/shader.rs:765`) + cup blow-up — exact "% of frame" not from a checked-in profiler table, so cited as "pressure-pair loop plausibly dominates" pending the `.deliberate/renders/` profiler artifact. `docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md`.
