     1	//! Headless GPU + CPU profiler for the MPM step pipeline.
     2	//!
     3	//! This is a measurement harness, not part of the shipped simulation. It runs
     4	//! the solver on the native (non-wasm) GPU backend exactly like
     5	//! [`physics_tests`](super::physics_tests), but re-encodes each solver pass into
     6	//! its own timestamped compute pass so per-pass GPU cost can be read back via
     7	//! `wgpu` timestamp queries. Aggregated results are written as JSON for
     8	//! offline bottleneck analysis.
     9	//!
    10	//! Run it (release is important — debug numbers are not representative):
    11	//!
    12	//! ```text
    13	//! cargo test -p coffee-sim-wasm --lib --release profile_mpm_pipeline -- --ignored --nocapture
    14	//! ```
    15	//!
    16	//! Tunable via environment variables:
    17	//! - `COFFEE_SIM_PROFILE_SCENE`   scene preset: `center_pour` (default) | `free_stream` | `water_block`
    18	//! - `COFFEE_SIM_PROFILE_SPARSE_PRESSURE`  `1`/`true` routes the pressure solve through the sparse tile RBGS path (default dense)
    19	//! - `COFFEE_SIM_PROFILE_WARMUP`  frames to run before measuring (default 60)
    20	//! - `COFFEE_SIM_PROFILE_FRAMES`  instrumented frames to measure (default 120)
    21	//! - `COFFEE_SIM_PROFILE_CAL`     production `step_frame` calibration frames (default 30)
    22	//! - `COFFEE_SIM_PROFILE_OUT`     output JSON path (default `<repo>/target/coffee-sim-profile.json`)
    23	//!
    24	//! ## Method and caveat
    25	//!
    26	//! Production [`MpmSim3D::step_frame`](super::MpmSim3D::step_frame) batches every
    27	//! solver pass into a single compute pass. To time individual passes, the
    28	//! instrumented path here opens a separate compute pass per pass type and writes
    29	//! GPU timestamps at the pass boundaries (`wgpu::Features::TIMESTAMP_QUERY`,
    30	//! which is broadly supported including on Metal stage-boundary sampling).
    31	//! Splitting passes can slightly inflate *absolute* GPU time versus production,
    32	//! but the MPM passes are almost entirely serialized by data dependencies, so
    33	//! the *relative* per-pass ranking — which is what identifies bottlenecks — is
    34	//! preserved. The Gauss-Seidel pressure solve keeps its red/black dispatches
    35	//! interleaved in one pass, matching production exactly. A separate calibration
    36	//! loop runs the real `step_frame` so the report also carries the honest
    37	//! production frame cost.
    38	
